### Browser automation

The project drives Chromium with [Playwright](https://playwright.dev/python/).
The dev container installs Playwright's Chromium for you; elsewhere, run
`uv run playwright install --with-deps chromium` once after `uv sync`.

Work out a flow headed, watching the browser, then run it headless once it
works:

```bash
uv run [[ project_name ]] --open https://example.com --pause      # headed; Enter to close
uv run [[ project_name ]] --open https://example.com --headless   # no window
uv run [[ project_name ]] --open https://example.com --trace run.zip
uv run playwright show-trace run.zip                              # replay a run, step by step
```

`src/[[ package_name ]]/browser.py` has the pieces to build on: `open_page()`
(headed by default, sized for each mode, with optional tracing) and `pause()`
to stop and look. For Playwright's own debugger, call `page.pause()`: it opens
the Inspector, where you step through the run and try out selectors. To
record a flow as code, run `uv run playwright codegen <url>`.

Browser tests use pytest-playwright's `page` fixture (`tests/unit/test_browser.py`).
They run headless; watch one with `uv run pytest --headed --slowmo 500`.

Headed runs need a display from your machine. On Windows, WSLg provides one,
including inside the dev container. To drive an installed Google Chrome instead
of Playwright's Chromium, pass `channel="chrome"` to `open_page()`.
